# ─── Verifier C ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This process's own file. Loaded BEFORE .env.local and .env, so anything here
# wins — put what belongs to this operator alone in it, and leave everything
# shared (chain, contract, storage, database) in .env.
#
# Read because VERIFIER_PROFILE=c is set when the worker runs.
#
# Copy to .env.verifier-c and fill in. Gitignored, like .env.

VERIFIER_C_PRIVATE_KEY=

# COMPUTE_DRIVER=zerog-router
# COMPUTE_API_KEY=sk-...
#
# The key is what the router bills, NOT this verifier's chain address — the two
# are unrelated accounts. Issue it on the network this deployment runs on:
# https://pc.0g.ai for mainnet (16661), https://pc.testnet.0g.ai for Galileo
# (16602). The catalogs and balances do not cross, and a key from the wrong one
# is rejected outright.
#
# Give each verifier its OWN key: the quota is per key, and one shared key would
# let a busy verifier starve the others — the one failure that makes verifiers
# agree by default instead of by evidence.

# C's own model, not the router default A and B run on. A fourth opinion from
# the same weights is not a fourth opinion. Whatever you put here has to pass
# the same three tests, all readable from `GET /v1/providers` (public, no key):
#
#   seedable   — the completion sends seed, and X-0G-Provider-Require-Parameters
#                makes the router refuse a provider that would drop it. Check
#                supported_parameters contains "seed".
#   TEE        — COMPUTE_VERIFY_TEE defaults to true; check tee_acknowledged.
#   parameters — temperature, top_p, max_tokens and response_format are sent on
#                every call, so supported_parameters must carry all four.
#
# Then confirm the id is listed by GET /v1/models, which is the route the driver
# probes on startup — not every id in /v1/providers is routable under that name.
# COMPUTE_MODEL=glm-5.3-flash

# Profile C defaults: depth 3, threshold 0.60 — the strictest of the four.
# VERIFIER_C_EVIDENCE_DEPTH=3
# VERIFIER_C_SUPPORT_THRESHOLD=0.60

# VERIFIER_MIN_COLLECT_WEI=2000000000000000
